i remember i was born eight years after  independence and india was still trying  to figure out who it was and trying to  figure out the role of women in society  so i think as a society it was very  conservative as it relates to women as  it related to women but in my family the  men in our family basically said we want  the women to dream we want them to so do  whatever they want and my poor mother  who was bound by social norms but still  wanted her kids to soar had one foot on  the brake and one foot in the  accelerator and the combination of my  grandfather father and my mother  were three feet on the brake i mean on  the accelerator and one foot on the  brake and that's why we were allowed to  do whatever we did  what was interesting though was that  when you joined pepsico  the the 15 top jobs were all held by  white american men but i get the  impression that they made you feel quite  welcome at least your face  i think that almost all of corporate  america  you know lack diversity because there  weren't enough diverse people who'd come  in  and risen in companies  they went out of their way to make me  feel welcome starting with the ceo and  every executive they were determined to  make sure that i had the support i had  the developmental opportunities and the  stretch assignments to thrive in pepsico  and i'm you know very grateful to what  they did because you know when you're in  such a senior position in a large  company and you're so different  um  you know it's tough to assimilate but  they made it a point to make sure that  they gave me a helping hand  now you were just the 11th woman to head  up a fortune 500  company in the first woman  of color the media were quite cruel to  you caricaturing you in a sorry even  though you hadn't worn one for many many  years  well you know they sort of pigeonholed  me and said you know i was sorry clad  and  they said that i was walking around bare  feet and singing  you know it was all an over exaggeration  because  i  never was sorry to work except in an  interview i had early in my days when i  left  yale  and uh i kicked off my shoes because the  heels heard after 5 p.m i thought that  was okay and i walked around bare feet  on the carpeted floor  and when you're exhausted at six or  seven in the night and you're still  working i hammed a tune i don't know  where this myth about the sorry clad  barefoot singing ceo suddenly became  larger than life but you know what it  made for good uh stories that's all  right  very very uh  almost obsessed these days with esg  issues but you were quite early on onto  the health and well-being piece at  pepsico and i gather it was because of  something that happened at your  daughter's birthday party  well you know  two things that happened one is when i  looked at the big mega trends that were  going to impact the consumer industry  and food and beverage companies in  particular  i realized that there were multiple  steps we had to take to future-proof the  company and one was the trend towards  health and wellness it was very  important that we transformed the  company to meet the changing consumer  and to change  to meet the changing environmental  requirements of businesses  but more importantly a more immediate  manifestation of that behavior was when  a young kid came to a birthday party and  she was dying to drink pepsi but she  said i've got to call and get permission  from my mother to have pepsi that hit me  because it said  not only is this trend something that's  happening over the next decade it's  actually begun to accelerate now  and for me to make sure that our company  stayed incredibly successful  i had to  future-proof this company by  transforming our portfolio into offering  a range of products that were good for  you better for you and fun for you so  darren started the big transformation of  the portfolio you also saw off the  activist investor nelson peltz not many  people do that  actually you know i saw two activist  investors and i think if you look at  what activist investors have to say  scrutinize their white paper  they actually give you free consulting  so you can if you find something in them  that  will help you run the company better  more power to us because we took a free  advice and did things that were better  for the company on the other hand if  they were trying to do something that  could perturb the company and really  cause it not to be successful in the  future we've got to put a stop to it  when that happens which is what i did  but i did it with respect  and ralph whitworth of relational stayed  a dear friend until his untimely passing  and nelson pels  remained a friend post the  activist  entry and exit from pepsico so i think  there's a way to deal with activists  with great respect and remain you know  good acquaintances with them  looking at the book it's obvious that  you've made a lot of sacrifices over the  years sort of imploring letters from  your daughters telling you to come home  early from work you missed a friend's  funeral on one occasion  are these the sort of things that hold  women back from the c-suite  i should hold men and women back because  i think child rearing is a joint  responsibility  and we've made it too much a female  responsibility as opposed to a family  responsibility and i think that in a  time when women are so smart they're  getting all the top grades they're  hungry they want to work they want to  contribute if the economy is going to  use the talents of everybody in the  economy we're going to have to find a  way for women to also engage in paid  work and contribute to the economy  because they represent an awesome talent  pool somebody once told me talented  women represent the greatest emerging  market opportunity in all our countries  within our countries  so i'm not saying they must i want to  give them the choice to go to work if  they so choose to and given that i think  it's important that  husband and wife both feel the loss of  not doing something with the kids on the  other hand i will say something to you  when you come home in your home all the  time the kids go off to watch tv or work  on their social media  and like wait a minute i thought you  wanted me home where are you oh mom we  just want you around  you know so it's one of those things  where  damned if you do damned if you don't  because  if you're home all the time it's like  mom's bugging us if you're not home it's  like i wish mom was home uh i think  nowadays they're beginning to say mom or  dad i think that's what we need to get  to yeah i completely uh  empathize with that  now one of the things that you're doing  now you're on the board of the  international cricket council do you  feel that uh tv revenues have gone too  much to australia england and india and  uh aren't being fairly distributed to  some of the other countries  well you know um this has been an  age-old problem for a long long time  india is a big big media market because  it's a crooked crazy country uh in a  nice way and uh you know next to  religion cricket is the most important  thing in india  so in a way india is good for cricket  because there's so many people  both within india and in the diaspora  who all want to watch cricket so whether  you like it or not india is going to be  a major force but india has to play  somebody they just can't keep playing  themselves  england and australia are hard currency  countries and so is new zealand  so they end up generating a lot of the  media revenue  all the other countries are very very  important to the icc because the end of  the day we're trying to build the  shoulders of the icc  and we are working hard on the board to  make sure that every one of those other  countries are financially viable and can  build a sport which can give us  15 20 test playing countries so that we  can keep the sport thriving into the  future both for men's cricket and for  women's cricket  in the short term it looks like  god these big countries are controlling  the agenda not the case on the icc board  responsibility is divided to include all  the countries and our goal is to  not just ensure that there's about 20  test playing countries but we also are  in the olympics in 2028 to really  broaden the viewership and the shoulders  of the game that's what we're focused on  right now

